<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abbas Mirza</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1789">1789</a>–<a href="/years/1833">1833</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abbas Mirza was the crown prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a>. He commanded Iranian armies in the Russo-Persian wars of 1804–1813 and 1826–1828, which ended with the loss of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-caucasus">South Caucasus</a> in the treaties of Gulistan and Turkmenchay. He tried to modernise the army on European lines and sent students to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, but died before his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fath-ali-shah-qajar">Fath Ali Shah</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abbas-mirza.jpg" alt="Abbas Mirza" width="320" height="390" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: L. HERR · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_Portrait_of_The_Crown_Prince_Abbas_Mirza,_Signed_L._Herr,_Dated_(1)833.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
